Chapter: GopZone
  cites Works chapter GopFat16

 Where the timezone lives, kept apart from both the clock pane and the
 wizard because both write it and neither should have to cite the other.
 The pane owns a widget tree and the wizard does not, so a shared home
 that cites only the filesystem is what keeps the wizard's dependency
 cone the size it is.

 The offset is a DISPLAY offset. It is deliberately never written to the
 MC146818: the top bar reads that part directly, and a zone that moved
 the hardware clock would make the two disagree about the instant every
 file on the stick is stamped with.

 The file is eight bytes on the ESP root, and the layout is fixed so a
 reader that is not this code can still tell what it found:

   0..3   magic 'C' 'T' 'Z' '1'
   4      offset plus twelve, so the byte is never negative
   5..7   zero

 Storing the offset biased is not decoration. A byte read back as a
 number is unsigned, so a stored -5 would return 251 and the clock would
 claim a zone that does not exist.

 We say:

Section: Range

  zone-bias : Integer = 12
  zone-min : Integer = 0 - 12
  zone-max : Integer = 14

  zone-clamp : Integer -> Integer
  zone-clamp (z) = if z < zone-min then zone-min else if z > zone-max then zone-max else z

  zone-pad2 : Integer -> Text
  zone-pad2 (v) = if v < 10 then "0" & show v else show v

  zone-text : Integer -> Text
  zone-text (z) = if z < 0 then "UTC-" & zone-pad2 (0 - z) else "UTC+" & zone-pad2 z

Section: The File

  zone-magic0 : Integer = 67
  zone-magic1 : Integer = 84
  zone-magic2 : Integer = 90
  zone-magic3 : Integer = 49

  zone-file-len : Integer = 8

 CTZ2 IS SIXTEEN BYTES AND ITS FIRST EIGHT ARE BYTE FOR BYTE WHAT CTZ1 WROTE.
 The magic does not change, which is the whole point: a CTZ1 reader takes the
 first eight bytes and gets the same whole-hour zone it always did, and a CTZ2
 reader that finds a short file takes the hour offset and defaults the rest.
 Neither has to know the other exists.

   8..9    offset in MINUTES plus 720, little endian
   10..11  zone id, little endian; 0 means "just the offset, no zone"
   12      clock format
   13      date format
   14..15  zero

 The minutes are biased for the same reason the hours are: a byte read back is
 unsigned, and a stored -300 would return as an enormous positive.

  zone2-len : Integer = 16
  zone2-bias : Integer = 720
  zone-min-minutes : Integer = 0 - 720
  zone-max-minutes : Integer = 840

  zone-clamp-minutes : Integer -> Integer
  zone-clamp-minutes (m) =
    if m < zone-min-minutes then zone-min-minutes
    else if m > zone-max-minutes then zone-max-minutes
    else m

 The whole-hour byte is the minutes ROUNDED and not truncated, because a
 truncating +05:30 writes +05:00 and a CTZ1 reader is then half an hour out
 rather than a whole one. Rounding away from zero on the half is what a person
 would do with a pencil.

  zone-hours-of : Integer -> Integer
  zone-hours-of (m) = if m >= 0 then (m + 30) / 60 else 0 - ((0 - m + 30) / 60)

  ZoneRec = record {
    zc-minutes : Integer,
    zc-id : Integer,
    zc-clock : Integer,
    zc-date : Integer,
    zc-band : Integer
  }

 What the taskbar clock shows, as a bit per part. It rides in byte 14, which
 CTZ2 has always written as zero and read as nothing, so a record written by
 an older build is not short and needs no version bump.

 STORED PLUS ONE, because zero in that byte means a build that never wrote it
 and "show nothing" is a legal mask. Without the offset a taskbar somebody had
 emptied on purpose and one from last week would be the same bits, and the
 desk would have to guess which.

  zone-band-time : Integer = 1
  zone-band-date : Integer = 2
  zone-band-offset : Integer = 4

  zone-band-default : Integer = 1

  zone-band-all : Integer = 7

  zone-rec-default : ZoneRec
  zone-rec-default = ZoneRec { zc-minutes = 0, zc-id = 0, zc-clock = 0, zc-date = 0, zc-band = zone-band-default }

  zone-name-bytes : Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  zone-name-bytes (buf) (s) (i) =
    if i >= text-length s then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (to-unicode (char-code (char-at s i)))
    in zone-name-bytes buf s (i + 1)

  zone-name : Integer
  zone-name =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let adv = __heap-advance 16
    in let w = zone-name-bytes b "TIMEZONEDAT" 0
    in b

Section: Saving

  zone-save : Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  zone-save (z) = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then False
    else
      let buf = __heap-save
      in let adv = __heap-advance 64
      in let a = poke-byte buf 0 zone-magic0
      in let b = poke-byte buf 1 zone-magic1
      in let c = poke-byte buf 2 zone-magic2
      in let d = poke-byte buf 3 zone-magic3
      in let e = poke-byte buf 4 (zone-clamp z + zone-bias)
      in let f = poke-byte buf 5 0
      in let g = poke-byte buf 6 0
      in let h = poke-byte buf 7 0
      in gfat-write-small-file (m.gm-vol) zone-name buf zone-file-len
  end

Section: The wider record

  zone2-save : ZoneRec -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  zone2-save (r) = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then False
    else
      let mins = zone-clamp-minutes (r.zc-minutes)
      in let buf = __heap-save
      in let adv = __heap-advance 64
      in let a = poke-byte buf 0 zone-magic0
      in let b = poke-byte buf 1 zone-magic1
      in let c = poke-byte buf 2 zone-magic2
      in let d = poke-byte buf 3 zone-magic3
      in let e = poke-byte buf 4 (zone-clamp (zone-hours-of mins) + zone-bias)
      in let f = poke-byte buf 5 0
      in let g = poke-byte buf 6 0
      in let h = poke-byte buf 7 0
      in let i = poke-byte buf 8 (int-mod (mins + zone2-bias) 256)
      in let j = poke-byte buf 9 ((mins + zone2-bias) / 256)
      in let k = poke-byte buf 10 (int-mod (r.zc-id) 256)
      in let l = poke-byte buf 11 (r.zc-id / 256)
      in let n = poke-byte buf 12 (r.zc-clock)
      in let o = poke-byte buf 13 (r.zc-date)
      in let p = poke-byte buf 14 (int-mod (bit-and (r.zc-band) zone-band-all + 1) 256)
      in let q = poke-byte buf 15 0
      in gfat-write-small-file (m.gm-vol) zone-name buf zone2-len
  end

 A SHORT FILE IS NOT A BROKEN ONE. Eight bytes is a CTZ1 record written by an
 older machine or by the wizard, and the honest reading of it is the offset it
 carries with everything else at its default. Refusing it would lose a setting
 the user made.

  zone2-load : [Device.Port] ZoneRec
  zone2-load = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then zone-rec-default
    else act
      f <- gfat-read-file (m.gm-vol) "TIMEZONE.DAT"
      if f.gf-ok == False then zone-rec-default
      else if f.gf-len < zone-file-len then zone-rec-default
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 0 /= zone-magic0 then zone-rec-default
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 1 /= zone-magic1 then zone-rec-default
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 2 /= zone-magic2 then zone-rec-default
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 3 /= zone-magic3 then zone-rec-default
      else
        let hrs = zone-clamp (peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 4 - zone-bias)
        in if f.gf-len < zone2-len then ZoneRec { zc-minutes = hrs * 60, zc-id = 0, zc-clock = 0, zc-date = 0, zc-band = zone-band-default }
        else
          let mins = zone-clamp-minutes (peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 8 + peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 9 * 256 - zone2-bias)
          in let bb = peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 14
          in ZoneRec {
            zc-minutes = mins,
            zc-id = peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 10 + peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 11 * 256,
            zc-clock = peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 12,
            zc-date = peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 13,
            zc-band = if bb == 0 then zone-band-default else bit-and (bb - 1) zone-band-all
          }
    end
  end

Section: Loading

 A stick with no timezone answers UTC, which is the only defensible
 default: it is the one offset that is never a guess about where the
 machine is.

  zone-load : [Device.Port] Integer
  zone-load = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then 0
    else act
      f <- gfat-read-file (m.gm-vol) "TIMEZONE.DAT"
      if f.gf-ok == False then 0
      else if f.gf-len < zone-file-len then 0
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 0 /= zone-magic0 then 0
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 1 /= zone-magic1 then 0
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 2 /= zone-magic2 then 0
      else if peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 3 /= zone-magic3 then 0
      else zone-clamp (peek-byte (f.gf-buf) 4 - zone-bias)
    end
  end

